Beyond the Individual

Currently, the world is facing a significant challenge in the form of climate change. As a geopolitical superpower, the US 
should be expected to lead the push to make the necessary economic changes to help solve the problem. So far, this has been lackluster.
If we want to live in a society that values environmental conservation, things most definitely need to change. 
This begs the question: what? What needs to change, and what could this lead to?

Questions 8-9 link: https://youtu.be/ICYVf0QfCzE

Synthesis:
I believe the reason that American society chafes so hard at the prospect of environmental regulation is rooted in the US's strong belief of individualism and personal freedoms. 
Environmental policy in action requires citizens to give up some degree of freedom.
In Dan Wang's book Breakneck, he argues that the gap between American and Chinese efficiency is rooted in who runs the state. 
He identifies the US as a lawyerly state and China as one run by engineers, and makes the case that this could be the root of US inefficiency. 
However, I disagree. 
The root of US inefficiency comes from friction between individual rights and environmental policy.
 	This issue with American individualism opposing Environmental regulation can be noted in a variety of ways. 
For example, Jonathan Woods notes that Endangered Species Act Consultation often holds up project development, even in cases where the project would benefit endangered species (Woods 2017). 
While this level of inefficiency should be criticized, I do not believe that is the only problem at hand. 
The issue with the ESA  can be seen in a variety of environmental regulations: it bumps heads with the individualistic beliefs that most Americans follow. 
U.S. culture has a very laissez-faire attitude towards economic development. 
As a result, regulations that are slow and complex will not work to serve the American public efficiently. 
Steven M. Teles argues that complex and incoherent policy makes it hard for the American public to understand its government and does not serve the people as well as it could (Teles 2023). 
In this same vein, complex environmental policy does not serve the American people if it consistently antagonizes developers and stalls projects.
 	In order to make the jump to an environmentally conscious nation, our regulations must reflect our principles. 
I am not advocating for the US to adopt weak regulation, but rather the opposite. 
I believe the US should discard complex regulation and instead develop harsh policy that makes environmental conservation the only possible alternative. 
This still allows for citizens to engage in whatever actions they see fit, but sets a firm and nonnegotiable boundary. 
Rather than burying people in complexity, the rules are clearly defined and understood. 
In order to govern an individualistic state, one needs individualistic policy.


